WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:04.460
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:08.540
We've seen a lot of fluid and smoke simulations
throughout the series.

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:13.400
In each of these cases, the objective was
to maximize the realism of these animations,

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:17.210
often to the point where they are indistinguishable
from reality.

00:00:17.210 --> 00:00:22.859
However, there are cases where creating photorealistic
footage is not the main objective.

00:00:22.859 --> 00:00:27.289
Artists often seek to imbue these fluid and
smoke simulations with their own distinctive

00:00:27.289 --> 00:00:30.730
style, and this style needs not to be photorealistic.

00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:35.890
It can be cartoonish, black and white, or
take a variety of different color schemes.

00:00:35.890 --> 00:00:41.100
But unfortunately, to obtain such an effect,
we have to sit down, get a bunch of papers,

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:44.950
and draw the entirety of the animation frame
by frame.

00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:49.600
And of course, to accomplish this, we also
need to be physicists and know the underlying

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:51.550
laws of fluid dynamics.

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:55.780
That's not only borderline impossible, but
extremely laborious as well.

00:00:55.780 --> 00:01:00.920
It would be really cool to have an algorithm
that is somehow able to learn our art style

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:04.489
and apply it to a fluid or smoke simulation
sequence.

00:01:04.489 --> 00:01:08.130
But the question is, how do we exactly specify
this style?

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:13.010
Have a look at this really cool technique,
I love the idea behind it: first we compute

00:01:13.010 --> 00:01:19.060
a classical smoke simulation, then, we freeze
a few frames and get the artist to colorize

00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:20.060
them.

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:25.070
After that, the algorithm tries to propagate
this artistic style to the entirety of the

00:01:25.070 --> 00:01:26.070
sequence.

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:31.590
Intuitively, this is artistic style transfer
for fluid animations, but, without using any

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:33.189
machine learning techniques.

00:01:33.189 --> 00:01:36.979
Here, we are doing patch-based regenerative
morphing.

00:01:36.979 --> 00:01:42.439
This awesome term refers to a technique that
is trying to understand the direction of flows

00:01:42.439 --> 00:01:48.250
and advect the colored regions according to
it in a way that is both visually and temporally

00:01:48.250 --> 00:01:49.670
coherent.

00:01:49.670 --> 00:01:54.840
Visually coherent means that it looks as close
to plausible as we can make it, and temporally

00:01:54.840 --> 00:02:00.360
coherent means that we're not looking only
at one frame, but a sequence of frames, and

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:05.009
the movement through these neighboring frames
has to be smooth and consistent.

00:02:05.009 --> 00:02:09.920
These animation sequences were created from
8 to 9 colorized frames, and whatever you

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:13.810
see happening in between was filled in by
the algorithm.

00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:18.360
And again, we're talking about the artistic
style here, not the simulation itself.

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:23.920
A fine, handcrafted work in the world dominated
by advanced learning algorithms.

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:30.630
This paper is a bit like a beautiful handmade
automatic timepiece in the era of quartz watches.

00:02:30.630 --> 00:02:34.480
If you enjoyed this episode, please make sure
to leave a like on the video, and don't forget

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:38.220
to subscribe to get a glimpse of the future
on the channel, twice a week.

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:42.320
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

